The Alert Center launched
about six months ago,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:17.010
and it's available for
all G Suite licenses.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:21.540
So that includes Basic,
Business, and Enterprise,

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:25.260
and it really provides
three key capabilities.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.570
It provides a
single pane of glass

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.970
of all your essential
security notifications.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.630
So this makes it easier
for you to save time

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:35.950
with everything in one place.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:39.060
It also gives you a view
of your overall security

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:43.350
exposure to help you prioritize
your team's workload,

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:46.170
as well as making
it easy and faster

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.710
to do remediation with
integration into other tools.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.410
The Alert Center has
several different types

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:59.620
of alerts across three
primary categories.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.430
The first category is
phishing and malware.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:06.180
As Chad talked about, that is
one of the most common threats

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.280
that we see, and
his slide earlier

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:12.240
showed about 91% of
cybersecurity starts in email.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.490
So this is an area that
we continuously invest in.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:20.550
So we show that 99.9% of
spam and phishing emails

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:22.830
get stopped before they
make it to the user,

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:28.020
but user attacks keep changing
over time and we keep learning.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.570
As we learn, we go
back and see if any

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.300
of the messages inside
your users' inboxes

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.850
have been affected and match new
patterns we've learned about,

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.660
and this is what we
call Reclassification.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:41.940
When this occurs, we
will automatically

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:45.150
move the emails from
your users' inboxes

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:48.150
and put them into the
Spam account for you.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.480
And if your users had already
engaged with those messages,

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.947
you will get a notification
in the Alert Center

00:14:53.947 --> 00:14:55.530
to let you know that
there's something

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.480
that you should go
investigate, and then you

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.060
can move it or delete
it for your user.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.610
The second category
is Account Warnings,

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.000
and this is where we're
using machine learning models

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.370
to detect suspicious
behavior that might

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.050
be occurring in that account.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.510
This is looking for
compromised accounts that

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.420
might have occurred
during login, or even

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:18.690
during in-app activity.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:20.640
And the third area
is Device Management

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.470
that Chad also touched upon.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.860
Here, we're automatically
looking at suspicious device

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.950
behavior, so if a
device has been rooted,

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.410
or it's jailbroken,
or device properties

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:33.420
have changed on that device.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.450
And one of the key goals of
the Alert Center, for us,

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.900
is to help you protect
your users, your devices,

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:40.560
and your data.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:45.750
Now, listening to
our customers is

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.820
one of the most
important things we do,

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:51.780
and it helps us make a
good product even better.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:54.600
We talked to several
hundred customers

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.300
where we received feedback
through surveys, interviews,

00:15:58.300 --> 00:15:59.920
and our alpha program.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.040
And I'd like to thank all
those alpha customers--

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.330
several are here today--

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.010
for their participation
in giving us

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.840
feedback across
the whole G Suite

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.610
family of security products.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.550
One of the common
themes that we heard

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.250
was the need for
collaboration, and this

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:18.410
was to make it easier for teams
to prioritize their workloads.

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:22.960
So with today's beta release,
we focused on these areas.

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:25.950
The first one is being
able to help you prioritize

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:30.510
automatically by giving each
alert type a different severity

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:31.470
setting.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.600
So Google has gone ahead
and mapped the severity

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.550
to each of the alerts,
but you, as your admin,

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:39.700
you can change those to
fit your business needs.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:42.840
The second is the
ability to track status.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:47.520
So you can now see if alerts
are new, in progress, or closed.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.210
And the third is to manage
workloads by assigning alerts

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.320
to different team members.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.220
So this enables faster
prioritization and more

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.740
efficient collaboration.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:00.240
So now, let's take
a look at a demo.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.770
So let me quickly log in.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:12.050
The first demo I'm
going to show you

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:15.050
is how easy it is to
roll into mobile device

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:16.355
management for your users.

00:17:24.648 --> 00:17:27.290
There we go.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:30.510
All right, so let me go to
Mobile Device Management.

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:32.640
So I'm going to
click on the menu

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.280
on the upper left-hand
corner, hit Devices, and go

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.190
to Mobile Devices.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.740
Let me log in to
my admin account.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.980
And on my device, I'm going
ahead and just logging

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.410
into my corporate email account.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.860
So I need to do a
couple clicks here.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.948
So just like any user,
I'm going to log in.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:06.510
Here, I just need to agree
to the terms of service,

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:09.040
and it's going to check
against the device policies

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:11.400
that you've set up as an admin.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:16.830
And I just need to agree to
enroll, so let me do that.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.730
And now here, in
the Admin console,

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.760
you will see that there
are about 10 devices,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.260
and this is all live.

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.090
I'm going to go
ahead and refresh,

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.550
and let's see if we see
the new device enrolled.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:30.300
So perfect, we see Good
Darshan's Pixel 2 Excel

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.330
that has just enrolled, and
now you have the ability

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:34.750
to manage this device.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.050
And this device is
following the policies

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:38.980
that you have already set.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:42.430
If you wanted, you can click on
the three-dot menu on the end,

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:45.270
and you can see some of the
features, like Wipe the Device

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:47.940
or Delete the Device, available.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.950
So that is showing
you just how easy it

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.010
is for a user to
automatically enroll and be

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:55.320
protected on the device front.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.570
Next, we're going to go
into the Alert Center.

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:59.790
So I'm going to click
on that same menu

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:02.715
and go to Security
and Alert Center.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:09.910
And here, you can see
there's several alerts

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:12.700
that the admins can see.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:14.440
And we're going to
pick the one that's

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:18.890
Device Compromised, so
let me open that one up.

00:19:21.588 --> 00:19:23.130
And now, in this
journey, we're going

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:26.150
to go through and see how
easy it is to triage messages

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:28.050
and prioritize them.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:31.010
So we can see that this
Android device is rooted

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.530
and it belongs to
darshan@dynamic70.

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:36.440
That happens to be the
device I had just enrolled,

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.880
and there's different
device properties there.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:42.260
And as we scroll down, we
can see this new section

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:44.025
called Related Alerts.

00:19:44.025 --> 00:19:45.650
So this is one of
the new beta features

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:48.530
that we just launched this
week, and what we're doing here

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.150
is we're looking
for other alerts

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:51.950
within the Alert
Center that might

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:56.750
have to do with this incident
to help you triage it better.

00:19:56.750 --> 00:19:59.890
So the first one here is
Phishing Message Detected

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:00.890
Post-delivery.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:02.540
So this looks
pretty interesting.

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:05.080
This is one of the examples we
had talked about earlier, so

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:07.760
let me click on that.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:09.860
And I can see
Google has detected

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:14.330
one reclassified message
from darshan@my.bigr.name.

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.250
So that looks pretty suspicious.

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:20.190
So let's go back to
the other message.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:23.270
Now we know that we have
a story coming together.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:25.050
Looking at the
next related alert,

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.300
I can see that is
leaked password.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.570
So this is another detection
we do automatically

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:30.750
on your behalf.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.430
We are looking at public sites
where your user's credentials

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:37.340
have been leaked, and when
we find those situations,

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.620
we automatically notify you
through the Alert Center

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.590
so you can work with your
users to remedy that situation.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:46.790
Another alert is
government-backed attacks.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.130
So again, we are
automatically detecting

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:51.770
if there might be a
state-sponsored initiative that

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:54.810
is trying to
compromise your users.

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:58.170
So it looks like there's a lot
of serious items going on here,

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.650
so let me scroll
back up to the top

00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:03.980
and prioritize and
assign this alert.

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:06.830
So I'm going to change
the severity to high

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:08.960
because there's a
lot going on here.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:12.080
I'm going to change the
status to In Progress

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:17.340
and assign it to
my colleague, Chad.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:22.570
And it automatically pre-fills
and let me apply that.

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:24.540
So again, just
within a few minutes,

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:29.820
we're able to show how easy
it is to triage and prioritize

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.000
an alert.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.635
Next, let's go back to
the Alert Center screen,

00:21:36.635 --> 00:21:38.260
and we're going to
pretend that now I'm

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:43.010
Chad, just a little shorter,
and go back in here.

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:46.030
And I see that there's
an alert assigned to me.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.610
We would have also
sent an email to Chad

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:55.460
saying that Darshan the admin
had assigned him an alert.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.950
So I'm going to open
this up, and you

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:01.140
can see in the upper
right-hand corner,

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:02.650
all these fields are there.

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:04.740
Again, these are all
part of the new beta.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:08.700
And if I scroll to the bottom
in the Alert History area,

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:11.100
you can see a change
log of everything that

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.550
has happened with this account.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:15.570
So teams can collaborate better
and know what has happened.

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:18.030
So let me go back up to the top.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:20.770
This is where we have the
integrated remediation.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:24.790
So as an admin, I could go
a couple different ways.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:27.552
One, I might try to figure
out how this happened,

00:22:27.552 --> 00:22:29.760
and we did have some leads
there through the phishing

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:32.453
and leaked password alerts.

00:22:32.453 --> 00:22:33.870
And the other one
might be to see,

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:35.412
are there any other
things that might

00:22:35.412 --> 00:22:38.370
have happened, such
as data exfiltration,

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:40.680
maybe sharing of
Drive documents?

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:45.620
So let's go and click on
the Investigate alert,

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:47.990
and this is part of the
seamless integration.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.940
And now I'm going to open
up the Investigation Tool.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:53.220
And in the
Investigation Tool, it

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.460
is pre-filling the
characteristics from the alert

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:00.130
into the Query Builder so you
don't have to do any of that.

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:02.160
And if I scroll
to the bottom, you

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:06.060
can see there's that result
for the compromised device.

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:08.640
Now, I'm going to
pivot to see if there

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:10.800
might have been any
data exfiltration that

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:12.370
occurred in Drive.

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:14.220
So by the Device
Owner, I'm going

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:16.450
to click on the three-dot menu.

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:18.420
And you'll see a
whole list of items--

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:20.580
let me scroll down
a little bit--

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:24.180
of different ways you can
pivot on this information,

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:27.910
and I'm going to select
Drive logs, Actor.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:31.530
And this is the look
where darshan@dynamic70

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:35.490
had done Drive log
events, or used Drive.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.800
And I'm going to
add a condition here

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:41.700
to see if the visibility
has been changed.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:43.650
And in particular,
we're looking to see

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:45.240
if the visibility changed--

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:48.453
let me pick that condition--
has changed externally.

00:23:48.453 --> 00:23:50.370
So we're going to look
to see if anything went

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:53.995
from internal to external,
and I'll scroll down and click

00:23:53.995 --> 00:23:54.495
on Search.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.730
It usually just
takes a few seconds.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:05.070
All right, and as
I scroll down, we

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.320
will see that there's
six documents that

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:10.380
have changed in Darshan's
account that have

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.400
gone from internal to external.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:15.120
And looking at the titles,
they look pretty sensitive.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:20.190
We see org charts for a secret
project, M&amp;A activity, PRDs,

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:21.540
sales projections.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:25.230
So let's go ahead and
click on the checkbox

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:30.460
to select all of them, and look
at what actions we can perform.

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:32.460
So here, we can
change the owners,

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:35.580
remove users, disable,
download, copy,

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:40.360
print, as well as go a little
deeper with file permissions.

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:43.550
So let's go ahead
and click on that.

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:45.770
And now it's going to
open up a new window where

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:48.650
you can see more details
on exactly who documents

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:50.180
have been shared with.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:53.000
In this case, we can
see the Project Zero Org

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.550
Chart has been shared mainly
with internal employees

00:24:56.550 --> 00:25:00.740
at Dynamic 70, but we also see
there is one external person

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:04.430
of Bad darshan@my.bigr.name.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.530
And this one, if you
may recall, happened

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.140
to be the same related
alert that was responsible

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:10.950
for the phishing email.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.580
So let me go ahead
and revoke privileges

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:16.790
by clicking on the menu,
and I can remove access

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:18.748
for Bad Darshan.

00:25:18.748 --> 00:25:20.665
But we also want to look
at all the documents,

00:25:20.665 --> 00:25:25.470
so let me click on the left side
to select all the documents.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:28.070
And I can click
on Actions, and I

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:31.300
can go ahead and select
which users I want to remove.

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.460
And I am going to remove
darshan@my.bigr.name.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:48.292
And now he has been removed from
access for all of these files.

00:25:48.292 --> 00:25:50.000
While we're on the
screen, we should also

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:52.300
go and check to see
if any documents might

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.740
have been shared publicly.

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:57.260
So I'm clicking on
links up at the top,

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.410
and I can see three
of the documents

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:01.160
have been made public.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:06.070
So let's go ahead and click on
the title, select all of those,

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:08.310
and edit the link sharing.

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:10.850
And let's make these
all restricted.

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:12.760
And I'm going to go
ahead and apply that.

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:18.110
All right, so just
within a few minutes,

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:22.220
we're able to go through three
common security use cases where

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:26.330
we can show how we help automate
and simplify the experience.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:28.370
The first one was
automatic enrollment

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:30.330
into mobile device management.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.350
The second one was
automatically looking

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:35.900
for incidents and
suspicious behaviors that

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:37.750
are occurring for your users.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.790
In this case, it was the
device being compromised.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:45.710
We also saw a leaked password
and a post-delivery phishing

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:48.650
reclassification,
as well as how easy

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:53.150
it is to triage, investigate,
and remediate a situation.

