00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:42.490
but this user was the
only one that opened it

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:44.620
and the only one that
clicked on the link.

00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:47.620
Just to make sure that there's
no more issues with this email,

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:51.340
we can come and we can delete
these messages to make sure

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:55.510
that none of our other
users click on this.

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:59.330
So we'll type that in
and we can click Delete.

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:01.150
So now what we can do is--

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:06.730
we've come in, we've
created the DLP rule.

00:33:06.730 --> 00:33:09.100
We've looked at where
that's triggered,

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:12.490
and then we've gone to better
understand how that user was

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:14.570
compromised in the first place.

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:18.280
But it looks like this
domain, ink42.com,

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:21.020
is targeting our users.

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:23.080
So we might want
to actually prevent

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:26.290
this from happening
again in the future.

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:27.790
What I want to
show you now is how

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:30.880
you can use the new
Activity Rules that we've

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.850
added to the Investigation
Tool to create policies

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:37.510
based on the attributes of
logs that you're receiving.

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:45.860
So we'll create a new query and
we'll say, Gmail log events.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:53.070
If it's from and it contains
Ink42, we want to block it.

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:55.850
And so we'll say,
specifically, when

00:33:55.850 --> 00:33:59.480
we receive emails from Ink42.

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:02.150
And then what we can do, right
within the Investigation Tool,

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.250
is we can come to
this three-dot menu

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:07.640
and we can go to Create Rule.

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:10.310
And that takes us to a
pretty similar experience

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:13.940
to what you saw for Data
Loss Prevention, or DLP.

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:17.674
And we can say,
block bad domain.

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:23.480
And then this pulls in
the query that we're

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:26.659
using in the
Investigation Tool itself.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:29.090
And then when we go
to the next step,

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:30.949
this will generate
a preview for us

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:33.860
for how often emails
from Ink42 have

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:35.420
come into our organization.

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:38.210
So you can see the history
of when that's happened.

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:41.989
And then we can say
if, within an hour,

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:43.580
it happens even zero times--

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:45.949
or if we want to set
a specific threshold,

00:34:45.949 --> 00:34:48.620
we can say more than
100 or more than 1,000.

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:50.120
So this works really
well for things

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.920
like document exfiltration.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.530
If you want to see if more than
a certain amount of documents

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:56.770
are shared outside
of your organization,

00:34:56.770 --> 00:34:59.810
this is a really flexible
tool that you can use.

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:02.360
Then we can set
an action and say,

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:04.730
if we receive an
email from Ink42,

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:06.590
I want to send it
to quarantine or I

00:35:06.590 --> 00:35:09.230
want to delete it,
whatever makes sense.

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:11.720
And then, we also want
to receive an alert,

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:15.080
and I want to send that
alert to the admin.

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:21.660
Just that quickly, we
can create a new rule,

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:26.180
which will then perform
that action whenever

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:27.780
